# The PAM stack the unit tests of `helper::server` run on, unless a test
# points its own helper at a directory of its own.
#
# It exists so that the suite tests THIS code rather than the machine it
# runs on. Without it a test's monitor would call pam_start("qshd", ...),
# find no /etc/pam.d/qshd, and fall back to /etc/pam.d/other -- which on a
# stock Ubuntu is @include common-account and @include common-session, so
# every channel test would end up asking logind for a session and reading
# /etc/shadow, unprivileged, and would pass or fail by the host's policy.
#
# pam_permit throughout, because none of those tests is about what a stack
# decides; the tests that ARE (a denied account, the session's environment,
# the open and close halves) write a stack of their own into a temporary
# directory and point one helper at it.
#
# `auth` is here although nothing calls pam_authenticate: pam_setcred runs
# the AUTH stack's setcred half, and a stack with no auth line at all would
# have nothing to run.
auth       required   pam_permit.so
account    required   pam_permit.so
session    required   pam_permit.so
